-- title:
--   Frobenius place clauses for π and w at p ∥ M
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, a nonzero modulus $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ whose image under the reduction map to $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is trivial (hypotheses `hpM`, `hpM2`, `hHp`). Fix also the hypothesis `hj`, that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`, the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}$-Laurent series generated by quotients of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of full level; `hj` is what makes available, for each congruence subgroup $\Gamma$, the two-chart integral model $X_p(\Gamma)$ = `X p Γ hj` over the base ring `R p` with structure morphism `toBase p Γ hj` to $\operatorname{Spec}(R p)$, its finite chart algebra `chartAlgFin p Γ hj` (the elements of `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ` integral over the subalgebra of `R p` adjoined with `jAt Γ hj`) and the corresponding chart morphism `ιFin p Γ hj` from the spectrum of that algebra into $X_p(\Gamma)$. The two levels occurring are `ΓM M H` and `ΓN p M H hpM`.
--
--   The data on the models over $\mathbb{Q}$ are: a morphism $\pi$ from $X_p(\Gamma_M)$ to $X_p(\Gamma_N)$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(R p)$; an `R p`-algebra homomorphism $\iota_0$ from the finite chart algebra at level $\Gamma_N$ to the one at level $\Gamma_M$ which by `iota0_spec` leaves the underlying Laurent series of each element unchanged, and which by `pi_chart` presents $\pi$ on the finite charts, in the sense that `ιFin p (ΓM M H) hj` followed by $\pi$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\iota_0$ followed by `ιFin p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj`; an isomorphism $w$ of $X_p(\Gamma_M)$ with itself satisfying $w \circ$ (source) $=$ `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj`, i.e. `w_over`; and an `R p`-algebra automorphism $\theta$ of the finite chart algebra at level $\Gamma_M$ which by `hwchart` presents $w$ on the finite chart, and which by `htheta` satisfies, for every $b$ in the finite chart algebra at level $\Gamma_N$, that the Laurent series of $\theta(\iota_0 b)$ is `qExpand ℚ p` applied to the Laurent series of $b$, that is, the substitution $q \mapsto q^p$.
--
--   The remaining data are indexed by the places above $p$: throughout, $A$ ranges over valuation subrings of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime p` (the image of $p$ lies in the nonunits of $A$) whose residue field $k_A$ has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed, and $\rho$ over ring homomorphisms from `R p` to $A$ whose composite with the inclusion of $A$ into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is the structure map of `R p` (hypothesis $h\rho$); write $\kappa =$ `residue` $\circ\, \rho$ for the induced map to $k_A$ and `fibre` for the pullback of `toBase` along $\operatorname{Spec} \kappa$. For each such $(A,\rho)$ the data comprise: a curve model `Mfib A hA ρ hρ` over $k_A$ of the field `qExpFunctionFieldC k_A (ΓN p M H hpM)` — that is, an integral scheme $C$ with a proper, smooth relative-dimension-one morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} k_A$, an isomorphism `ffEquiv` of that field with the function field of $C$ compatible with the base, and a bijection `placeOfPoint` of the closed points of $C$ with the places of the field over $k_A$ whose stalks cut out the corresponding valuation subrings, every finite set of points lying in an affine open; a morphism `efib A hA ρ hρ` from the curve model to the level-$\Gamma_N$ fibre, assumed an isomorphism (`efib_iso`), compatible with the projections (`hefib`: `efib` followed by `pullback.snd` is the model's base morphism); the nonemptiness hypothesis `Mfib_chart_nonempty`, that the preimage under `efib` followed by `pullback.fst` of the open image of the finite chart at level $\Gamma_N$ is nonempty; and the normalisation `Mfib_pin`, which asserts for every element $b$ of the finite chart algebra at level $\Gamma_N$ and every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ with `coeffMap A.subtype y` equal to the image of the Laurent series of $b$ under `coeffEmb`, that transporting $b$ through the chart's section isomorphism, the sections map of `efib` followed by `pullback.fst`, the germ at the generic point and `ffEquiv.symm` yields the element of `qExpFunctionFieldC k_A (ΓN p M H hpM)` whose Laurent series is the coefficientwise reduction `coeffMap (residue) y`.
--
--   Further, for each $(A,\rho)$ a pair of morphisms `comp A hA ρ hρ i`, $i \in \{0,1\}$, from the level-$\Gamma_N$ fibre to the level-$\Gamma_M$ fibre is given, subject to: `comp_over`, each composite with `pullback.snd` is `pullback.snd`; `comp_isClosedImmersion`, each is a closed immersion; `comp_pi`, `comp … 0` followed by `fibreMap π κ` is the identity; and `comp_w`, `comp … 0` followed by `fibreMap (overOfIso w w_over) κ` is `comp … 1`. Finally `hfin` assumes, for each $(A,\rho)$, that `FiniteAlong k_A (qExpFrobeniusModL k_A (ΓN p M H hpM) p)` holds, i.e. `qExpFunctionFieldC k_A (ΓN p M H hpM)` is a finite module over itself via the $p$-power substitution $k_A$-algebra map.
--
--   Under these hypotheses both of the following hold.
--
--   First: for every $A$, $\rho$ as above and every closed point $P$ of the curve model $C$, the image of $P$ under the base map of `efib` followed by `comp … 1` followed by `fibreMap π κ`, transported back by the base map of the inverse of `efib`, is again a closed point of $C$ (the closedness is the existential witness in the Lean statement), and the place attached to it by `placeOfPoint` equals `qExpFrobeniusPlaceModL k_A (ΓN p M H hpM) p` applied to `placeOfPoint P`, that is, the restriction of that place along the $p$-power substitution endomorphism of the function field.
--
--   Second: the same assertion with `comp … 1` replaced by `comp … 0` followed by `fibreMap (overOfIso w w_over) κ`: for every $A$, $\rho$ and every closed point $P$ of $C$, the point obtained by applying the base map of `efib` followed by `comp … 0`, `fibreMap (overOfIso w w_over) κ` and `fibreMap π κ` to $P$ and pulling back along the inverse of `efib` is a closed point of $C$, and its place under `placeOfPoint` is `qExpFrobeniusPlaceModL k_A (ΓN p M H hpM) p` of `placeOfPoint P`.
--
--   These are the two Frobenius clauses of the Deligne–Rapoport description of $X_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing $M$: on the component of the special fibre picked out by `comp … 1` (equivalently, the image of `comp … 0` under the Atkin–Lehner involution $w$), the degeneracy map $\pi$ induces on places the $p$-power substitution $q \mapsto q^p$, i.e. the geometric Frobenius. The result supplies these two fields to the construction of the integral model bundle used in [`ModularCurve.exists_xHDRModelAtP_atkinLehner_generic_chart`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_xHDRModelAtP_atkinLehner_generic_chart).
